The shared-rational-side trick needs more than conditional independence alone. I can state sufficient moments for it: a polynomial-loss second moment for the rational candidate count, a conditional algebraic mean on good moduli, and an averaged algebraic second moment with loss \(\exp(o(\Lambda_\alpha))\). Conditional Paley–Zygmund and Markov then give an amplification argument.

For LV6, factorial-length character primes do repair the stated gap at subleading cost. Thorner–Zaman’s 2019 relative-error theorem also gives a polynomial-bit alternative, though a factorial number of character tests can remain.
