  Blake–Fuji-Hara–Mullin–Vanstone u/v trick. We found no rigorous ratio-form statement for composite N
  restricted to square classes; that is a search result.
- The fourth power is the price of the crude multiplicity bound. For smooth-type S one can use
  Ψ(x/d, y) ≪ d^{−α}Ψ(x, y) to recover |S|²·L^{o(1)}. Not needed here.
- E1x (§7) checks the bound exactly in 120 small cases. The true value is about |S|²/φ(N) for random S
  and about 2^ω|S|²/φ(N) when S ⊂ Q_N. So the bound is far from sharp but has the right structure.

**Theorem S′ (k-product sets) [proved].** Let G be a set of primes in a dyadic interval, coprime to N, with
|G| ≥ 4k². Let S be the products of exactly k distinct primes of G lying in a window [Y/2, Y] (Y² < N),
with |S| ≥ C(|G|, k)/(2k). Then Pr[E_S] ≥ |S|²/(c_k·2^ω·φ(N)) with c_k = 8k²·C(2k, k).

*Proof.* As in Theorem S, P ≥ |S|²/h. Split Σ_R n(R)² ≤ Σ_{(u,v)∈S²} #partners(u,v) according to
i = the number of shared primes, so (u, v) = (ju₀, jv₀) with j an i-product.
1. There are at most C(|G|,i)·C(|G|,k−i)² such pairs.
2. Each has at most C(|G|,i) partners j′(u₀, v₀).
3. So the i-th term is ≤ C(|G|,i)²C(|G|,k−i)² ≤ 2C(k,i)²C(|G|,k)² ≤ 8k²C(k,i)²|S|². The middle step uses
   C(n,i)C(n,k−i) = C(k,i)C(n,k)·∏_{t<i}(n−t)/(n−k+i−t) ≤ C(k,i)C(n,k)·e^{k²/(n−k)} ≤ √2·C(k,i)C(n,k) for n ≥ 4k².
4. Summing, Σ_i C(k,i)² = C(2k,k), so Σn(R)² ≤ c_k|S|². Then use Cauchy–Schwarz as before. ∎

This is the |S|² ("λ = 2") form, the Dixon–Pomerance k-product lemma in ratio form. Theorem K(ii)
improves accordingly, to Pr ≥ c′_k·(g_A/(4 log N))^{2k}·2^{−ω} for |G_A| ≥ 4k². E1x (random S) sits at
about 0.9·|S|²/φ(N).

## 3. Theorem K: bounded-k splits reduce Hypothesis D to one averaged statement

**Setup.**
- Fix integers k ≥ 1 and odd d ≥ 3. Let X = ⌊√(N/2)⌋.
- Let j be maximal with 2^{kj} ≤ X, and put J = (2^{j−1}, 2^j], so ℓ ∈ J means ℓ = N^{1/(2k)+o(1)}.
- For a field A, let G_A ⊂ J be the set of primes ℓ ∤ N for which a fixed, deterministic,
  capped special-q search succeeds. The search depends only on (A, ℓ), with time cap T_SQ·L^{o(1)}. It
  succeeds when it finds a primitive (a, b) ∈ Λ_ℓ(m) in the box with (a − bm)/ℓ and F_A(a, b) both
  B-smooth.
- Let g_A = |G_A|/π_J with π_J = #(primes in J), and γ := E_A g_A = Pr_{A, ℓ∈J}[ℓ ∈ G_A].

**Hypothesis D_k(γ).** γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}, uniformly in N. The search box and the field law are those of §4.

**Theorem K [proved, given the setup].**
- (i) Pr_A[g_A ≥ γ/2] ≥ γ/2.
- (ii) Fix a field A with |G_A| ≥ 4k², and let S_A be the products of exactly k distinct primes of
  G_A. The hypothesis on |G_A| was missing; codex fix. Then for N ≥ N₀(k),
    Pr_R[R ≡ u/v with u, v ∈ S_A] ≥ c_k·(g_A/(4 log N))^{4k}·2^{−ω}/(1 + log N), with c_k > 0 depending
    only on k.
- (iii) **[corrected after review]**
  - Acceptance rule (codex fix). Accept R when the primitive split (u₀, v₀) has each side a product of
    **at most** k distinct primes of G_A. "Exactly k" is not divisor-closed: cancelling gcd(u, v) can
    remove primes.
  - The count in (ii) still lower-bounds acceptance, because the primitive part of an S_A-pair is
    accepted.
  - In a field with g_A ≥ γ/2 and |G_A| ≥ 4k², a descent attempt succeeds after an expected
    L^{o(1)}·γ^{−2k} R-trials.
  - Each trial must split u₀, v₀ ≈ √N into their prime factors, all of which are ≈ N^{1/(2k)}. This is
    general factoring of √N-size integers, **not** a smoothness test.
  - HECM would cost L_{N^{1/(2k)}}[2/3] = L_N[2/3, c(2k)^{−2/3}]. The first version wrote "L_N[1/3]",
    which is wrong.
  - We use Lenstra–Pomerance's rigorous algorithm instead, at T_shape = L_{√N}[1/2, 1] = L_N[1/2, 1/√2]
    per trial.
  - Each trial with the right shape adds at most 2k special-q searches.
- So the expected cost per descent relation is (T_shape + T_SQ)·L^{o(1)} whenever γ ≥ L^{−o(1)}.

*Proof.*
- (i) Since 0 ≤ g_A ≤ 1, we have γ ≤ γ/2 + Pr[g_A ≥ γ/2].
- (ii)
  1. All products of k distinct primes of G_A lie in (2^{k(j−1)}, 2^{kj}] ⊂ (X·2^{−2k}, X]. By the
     pigeonhole principle, one of the 2k dyadic windows [Y/2, Y] with Y ≥ X·2^{−2k} holds at least
     C(|G_A|, k)/(2k) of them.
  2. Distinct prime sets give distinct products, so |S_A ∩ [Y/2, Y]| ≥ (|G_A|/k)^k/(2k).
  3. Use π_J ≥ 2^{j−1}/(2(j−1)log 2) for j ≥ j₀ (Rosser–Schoenfeld) and Y ≤ 2^{kj}. Then
     |S|/Y ≥ (g_A/(4kj log 2))^k/(2k), and 4kj log 2 ≤ 2 log N.
  4. Apply Theorem S with Y² ≥ X²·2^{−4k} ≥ N·2^{−4k−2}.
- (iii) Average over R. The bounded factors (log N)^{O(k)}, 2^{O(k)} and 2^ω are L^{o(1)}, by Lemma Ω.
  The shape test costs T_shape per trial. Conditional on u₀v₀ having the right shape, at most 2k
  special-q searches are run. Pr[shape]/Pr[success] ≤ (log N)^{O(k)}g_A^{−4k}2^{2ω} = L^{o(1)} (upper-bound Pr[shape] by 2·2^ω|S_J|²/φ(N), S_J = all k-products from J), so
  wasted searches cost only L^{o(1)} on average. ∎

**Corollary K [proved implication, modulo the LV §6 adaptation that lemmaM Corollary DD also leaves
unwritten; corrected after review].** Assume D_k(γ), for k ≥ (3/4)(d+1) (FM's rational-side
range). Then every N is factored in expected time L[1/2, (1+θ(d,k))/√2 + o(1)] for θ < 1, where
  θ(d,k) = 2s + (d−1)/(4k) + max(1/(4k), s − (d−2)/(4k)), s = 1/(d+1).
- The floor 1/(4k) on h comes from LV's kernel mechanism, which needs H ≫ ‖(a,b)‖ (critic fix). It
  matters only for k < (d²−1)/4.
- *Algorithm.*
  1. Field selection (codex repair, replacing an ill-posed pilot and then a guess-and-double that
     could waste a main-scale factor).
  Σ_{m′≤Q} Σ_{(a,m′)=1} |Σ a_F e(aF/m′)|² ≤ (K + Q²)Σ|a_F|².
  - Montgomery–Vaughan, Mathematika 20 (1973), Thm 1 (in the form N + δ^{−1}).
  - The sharp K − 1 + Q² is due to Selberg and to Montgomery–Vaughan (with P. Cohen); see Montgomery,
    Bull. AMS 84 (1978) 547–567.
- **(DS)** pcest Lemma DS: every residue, non-reduced included; modulus ≤ B^{C₀}; intervals of length
  ≥ XB^{−C₀} inside [XB^{−C₀}, X].
- **(Hi)** ϱ(z₂) ≤ 2ϱ(z₁) for z₂ ≥ z₁. **(C8.16)** ϱ(x) = ϱ(xB^c)(log x)^{O(⌈c⌉)} for fixed c ≥ 0.

Fix C₀ such that R₀ := B^{C₀} ≥ 64S²·L/(ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)) for large N. This is possible since all three factors
are L^{O(1)}. Note R₀ < Y.

**Theorem L2_fam [proved].** Uniformly for 1 ≤ q ≤ 2S²,
  (***)  E_ℓ Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} Σ_{m′ | ℓ²|n|q, m′>1} f(m′) ≤ τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}·Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)².
Consequently, with the envelope V₋,
  E_ℓ Σ_{n∈𝒩_ℓ} 𝔔(ℓ²|n|q)/φ(ℓ|n|) ≤ τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}·Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)²/(Y q (MV₋)²).   (4.1)

*Proof.* Recall ℓ > max(B, 2S²) ≥ max(P⁺(n), q), so ℓ ∤ |n|q.
- **Rearrangement.** f(m′) does not depend on ℓ, because X* is common to all ℓ ∈ J. So the left side of
  (***) is Σ_{m′>1} f(m′)ν(m′), with ν(m′) := E_ℓ #{n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ : m′ | ℓ²|n|q}.
- **Partition (independent of ℓ).**
  - (A): m′ has no prime factor in J, and 1 < m′ ≤ R₀.
  - (B): m′ has no prime factor in J, and m′ > R₀.
  - (C): m′ has a prime factor in J.
  - Since R₀ < Y, the classes are disjoint and exhaustive.
  - For (A) and (B), m′ | ℓ²|n|q ⟺ m′ | |n|q, for every ℓ.
  - For (C), m′ | ℓ²|n|q forces ℓ | m′. Then ℓ is m′'s unique J-prime factor, with exponent i ∈ {1, 2}. An m′
    with two J-prime factors has ν = 0.
- **(A).**
  - *Bound on f.* f(m′) ≤ Σ_{a mod m′}|S(a/m′)|² = m′Σ_c N_c², with N_c := #{F ∈ 𝒮* : F ≡ c (mod m′)}. DS on
    ±[X*R₀^{−1}, X*], plus the trivial bound X*R₀^{−1}/m′ + 1 on |F| < X*R₀^{−1}, gives
    N_c ≤ (X*/m′)ϱ(X*)(log N)^{O(1)}. This uses R₀^{−1} ≤ ϱ(X*) and m′ ≤ X*ϱ(X*). Hence
    f(m′) ≤ m′·max_c N_c·#𝒮* ≤ Ψ(X*)²(log N)^{O(1)}.
  - *Bound on ν.* Let m₂ := gcd(m′, q) and m₁ := m′/m₂. Then m′ | |n|q ⟺ m₁ | n, since gcd(m₁, q/m₂) = 1.
    If m₁ is not B-smooth, ν = 0. Otherwise, by (Hi) and (C8.16),
    ν ≤ 2Ψ(X′₊/m₁) ≤ 4(X′₊/m₁)ϱ(X′₊R₀^{−1}) ≤ (Ψ(X′₊)/m₁)(log N)^{O(C₀)}.
  - The map m′ ↦ (m₁, m₂) is injective, with m₂ | q. So Σ_{m′≤R₀} 1/m₁ ≤ τ(q)(1 + log R₀).
  - Hence (A) ≤ τ(q)(log N)^{O(1)}Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)².
- **(B).**
  - Here m′ ≤ X′₊q. On dyadic blocks (R, 2R], R = R₀2^i: ν(m′) ≤ #{1 ≤ |n| ≤ X′₊ : m₁ | n} ≤ 2X′₊/m₁ ≤ 2X′₊q/R.
  - By (LS) with K = 2⌊X*⌋ + 1 and Q = 2R: Σ_{m′∈(R,2R]} f(m′) ≤ (3X* + 4R²)·3Ψ(X*).
  - So (B) ≤ 36X′₊qX*Ψ(X*)/R₀ + 192X′₊²q²Ψ(X*).
  - First term: 36X′₊qX*Ψ(X*)/R₀ ≤ Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)², because R₀ ≥ 36q/(ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)).
  - Second term: 192X′₊q² ≤ ϱ(X′₊)ϱ(X*)X*, because X*/X′₊ ≥ c_dHV₋^{d−1}Y ≥ N^h and q²/(ϱϱ) = L^{O(1)}.
- **(C).**
  - Only ℓ = ℓ(m′) contributes, so ν(m′) = π_J^{−1}#{n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ : m″ | |n|q}, where m″ := m′/ℓ^i.
  - On a block (R, 2R] with Y/2 ≤ R ≤ 4Y²X′₊q (O(log N) blocks), two bounds hold: ν ≤ 3Ψ(X′₊)/π_J, and, since
    m″ > R/(4Y²), ν ≤ 8X′₊qY²/(π_JR).
  - Using the first bound against 3X* and the second against 4R²:
      (C) ≤ π_J^{−1}[27 log N·Ψ(X′₊)X*Ψ(X*) + 192X′₊qY²·8Y²X′₊q·Ψ(X*)].
  - First term: X*/π_J ≤ Ψ(X*)/log² N, since π_J = N^{2ν+o(1)} and 1/ϱ(X*) = L^{O(1)}.
  - Second term: it suffices that X′₊q²Y³ log Y ≤ c·ϱϱ·X*. Since X*/X′₊ ≥ c_dHV₋^{d−1}Y, this follows from
    h + (d−1)ν > 4ν, i.e. h > (5−d)ν. The Kern floor h ≥ 2ν + η₀ implies this with margin, for all d ≥ 3.
- **(4.1).**
  - For n ∈ 𝒩_ℓ: m := ℓ²|n|q ≥ ℓqMV₋/32 and φ(ℓ|n|) ≥ cMV₋/log log N.
  - So 𝔔(m)/φ(ℓ|n|) ≤ C log log N·[(#𝒮*)² + Σ_{m′|m, m′>1} f(m′)]/(Yq(MV₋)²).
  - Sum over n, average over ℓ, and use #𝒩_ℓ ≤ 3Ψ(X′₊) and (***). ∎

(Exponent audit by codex ldbound_2, ratios to the target Ψ(X′₊)Ψ(X*)²:
- (B) first term: q/(R₀ρ_rρ_F);
- (B) second term: N^{−h−(d+1)ν}L^{O(1)};
- (C) first term: N^{−2ν}L^{O(1)};
- (C) second term: N^{(5−d)ν−h}L^{O(1)}.
All are small. `params.margins` checks the exponents.)

*Remarks.*
- **What is used.**
  - No Siegel–Walfisz input and no equidistribution are used.
  - The pointwise DS bound covers conductors ≤ B^{C₀} for any fixed C₀.
  - The large sieve's diagonal X*Ψ(X*) is beaten in two ways: by the divisibility sparsity
    ν(m′) ≲ X′₊q/m′ in class (B), and by the average over N^{2ν} primes ℓ in class (C).
- **The ℓ-average is essential in (C).**
  - For one fixed ℓ, the frequencies with denominator ℓ² contribute ℓ²Σ_c N_c² over classes mod ℓ². Its
    trivial bound is X*Ψ(X*) ≫ Ψ(X*)².
  - A single-modulus bound would need smooth numbers to be L²-equidistributed mod ℓ² = B^{Θ(u)}.
  - That is unconditionally out of reach: the primes ≤ B could lie in a subgroup of (Z/ℓ²)^× of index up
    to 1/ϱ(ℓ², B) = L^{Θ(1)}, since Burgess-type bounds are far above B = ℓ^{o(1)} (cf. Soundararajan,
    CRM Proc. 46 (2008); Harper 2012).
- **Codex's blind proof is different.** Its Theorem AR (`notes/ldbound_codex1_report.md` §3) proves the
  needed bound, in a harmonic q-average form (not uniformly in each q), through multiplicative characters.
  It uses induced-character multiplicities, Harper's Prop. 1
  and zero-density results for the ℓ, ℓ² conductors, and exact gcd layers for non-reduced classes. Its
  worst exponent is the same, h + (d−5)ν > 0. The additive route above avoids the induced-character
  bookkeeping.
- **Prop B5 is not contradicted.** L2_fam is an L² statement over all classes. It never passes through
  max-class ratios.
- **Attribution (literature critic).**
  - The route is the classical large-sieve route to Barban–Davenport–Halberstam upper bounds (Gallagher,
    Mathematika 14 (1967); Montgomery, Michigan Math. J. 17 (1970)).
  - The rearrangement Σ f(m′)·#{moduli divisible by m′} is in the spirit of Hooley's switching
    (J. reine angew. Math. 274/275 (1975)) and of Harper 2025's "non-concentration on multiples".
  - Smooth-number BDH-type bounds over *all* moduli: Harper, arXiv 1208.5992, Thm 2 (preprint), with
    saving H(u)^{−c}. It does not imply L2_fam: the family's main term is Ψ²·L^{−Θ(1)}, below Harper's
    error term Ψ²L^{−o(1)}.
  - Upper bounds of this type over all moduli are folklore: Granville, MSRI Publ. 44 (2008) §4.2,
    attributes one to Granville–Vaughan.
  - Harper, J. London Math. Soc. 112 (2025) e70293, Cor. 1 requires x^{0.51} ≤ Q ≤ x and does not apply.
  - We found no statement of L2_fam itself. What is specific here is the restricted family, the L^{o(1)}
    bookkeeping and the ℓ-average.

