**The apparent \(\tau=1\) barrier is removable.** After matching the smoothness densities and repairing the geometric bounds, the listed moment ingredients admit \(\tau=0\) at the \(2/5\) boundary. The shared-rational-side construction also has a valid amplification argument, but conditional independence alone does not establish it.

I distinguish two claims throughout:

- **Proved below, relative to the supplied internal chain:** the required uniformity repairs and their consequences for that framework.
- **Established in the literature:** no verified classical factoring bound \(L_N[\alpha,c]\), \(\alpha<1/2\), for arbitrary \(N\). The project’s internal chain remains unrefereed.

The files as written do not state the growing-degree theorem proved by the repairs below.

**1. Precision bookkeeping**

Write
\[
T=\log N,\qquad \lambda=\log\log N,\qquad D=T/\lambda,\qquad
\Lambda_\alpha=D^\alpha\lambda.
\]
Use \(\beta(x,B)\) for the saddle point, reserving \(\alpha\) for the complexity exponent.

At the balanced scales in the question,
\[
\log x\asymp D^{2\alpha}\lambda,\qquad
\log B=aD^\alpha\lambda,\qquad
u=\frac{\log x}{\log B}\asymp D^\alpha.
\]
Consequently,
\[
1-\beta(x,B)\asymp D^{-\alpha},\qquad
\log\frac1{\varrho(x,B)}=\Theta(\Lambda_\alpha).
\tag{1}
\]

This immediately settles an important normalization issue. For the shifts occurring here, the local smooth-number estimates give
\[
\log\frac{\varrho(x/z,B)}{\varrho(x,B)}
=(1-\beta(x,B))\log z+o(1)
\]
when the displayed main term tends to zero; bounded-factor versions suffice elsewhere. These are consequences of Hildebrand–Tenenbaum’s local ratio theorem; see their 1993 survey, Corollary 1.7. [Hildebrand–Tenenbaum](https://www.numdam.org/item/JTNB_1993__5_2_411_0.pdf)

For \(d\asymp D^{1-2\alpha}\), \(\alpha\ge2/5\), this yields:

| Shift of the argument | Effect on \(\varrho\) |
|---|---|
| \(2^{O(d)}\) | \(1+o(1)\) |
| \(d^{O(d)}\) | \(1+o(1)\) |
| \(B^c\), fixed \(c\) | \((\log N)^{O(c)}\) |
| \(B^{O(d)}\) | potentially \(\exp(O(d\log\log N))\) |

Thus an exponential factor **in a norm bound** need not be an exponential precision loss. An exponential factor **multiplying an upper moment after normalization** is different.

Also, the \(S^d\) contribution must be retained in both occurrences of the algebraic density:
\[
X_F\asymp dMH V_{\max}^d.
\tag{2}
\]
Dropping \(S^d\) from only the lower-bound normalization manufactures a \((\log N)^{O(d)}\) discrepancy.

For
\[
\mathbb EN\ge m/P,\qquad
\mathbb E[N(N-1)]\le Qm^2,\qquad
m=S^2\rho_r\rho_F,
\]
the correct conclusion is
\[
\gamma\ge \frac1{P/m+QP^2}.
\tag{3}
\]
Only \(Q\), \(P\), and whether \(m\) is sufficiently large matter. Common smoothness-density factors cancel.

**1(a). FM: \(\tau=0\), after an explicit uniform rewrite.**

The LV-type coefficient argument has polynomial dimension dependence:

- The small representative construction for \(\phi_P\) has coordinate bound \(O(V)\), independent of \(d\). Its exponentially large cardinality is not a probability loss or an enumeration cost.
- Convolving the \(d\) small kernels produces displacement \(O(dV)\).
- The boundary error is \(O(d^2V/H)\).
- Flattening costs \(O(\omega^{-1})\).
- The displacement of \(\widehat F\) costs
  \[
  O\!\left(\exp(O(d\log d))\,\omega N^{-\eta_0/2}\right)
  \tag{4}
  \]
  using even the coarse coefficient bounds.

Take \(\omega=B^C\), with fixed \(C\) larger than a uniform upper bound for \(\log(1/\rho_F)/\log B\). All these errors are \(o(\rho_F)\) provided
\[
\eta_0T\gg \Lambda_\alpha+d\log d+\log d.
\tag{5}
\]
The parameter choice given below satisfies this with substantial room.

For every good rational modulus, the uniform-piece argument then gives
\[
\Pr_{w,c}\bigl(F(P)\text{ is }B\text{-smooth}\mid g,P\bigr)
\ge \rho_F/(\log N)^C,
\tag{6}
\]
uniformly in \(d,k\), with an absolute exponent after fixing the scaled parameters.

The remaining losses are prime-\(b\) density, coprime-fibre density, the grid size, and fixed powers of \(\log N\). Narrowing the \(b\)-window as below adds only a polynomial factor in \(d\).

One correction to the old fibre proof is necessary: replace “\(2^{\omega(t)}=N^{o(1)}\)” by the uniform estimate
\[
2^{\omega(t)}\le
\exp\!\left(O\!\left(\frac{\log t}{\log\log t}\right)\right).
\]
Here \(\log(M/V)\asymp\log t\), so the Möbius error remains negligible.

Hence
\[
\mathbb EN\ge
\frac{S^2\rho_r\rho_F}{d^{O(1)}(\log N)^C}.
\tag{7}
\]

**Status:** proved by this quantitative version of the internal FM argument, using the restricted BV result below. Published LV alone supplies neither this precision statement nor the special-\(q\) second moment. Its relevant coefficient arguments are Lemmas 5.13, 5.18 and 5.20; its Theorem 5.4 is a first moment. [Lee–Venkatesan](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

**1(b). Lemma 8.5′: the proposed regime still admits \(\tau=0\).**

The essential observation is
\[
\log B\asymp\sqrt{\log x\,\log\log x}.
\tag{8}
\]
Thus \(B\) is an \(L_x[1/2,\text{constant}]\) bound, even though it is \(L_N[\alpha,a]\).

Here is a uniform formulation sufficient for FM. Assume fixed \(\lambda_0>0\), fixed sufficiently large \(K\), and
\[
B^K\le Y,\qquad x^{\lambda_0}Y\le Q\le x^{1/2-\lambda_0}.
\tag{9}
\]
Then the proof in `lem85_results.md` works with
\[
\varepsilon_N=
\exp\!\left(-c\frac{u}{\log^2(u+1)}\right)+B^{-c}
+x^{-\lambda_0/2}.
\tag{10}
\]
In particular,
\[
\sum_{t\in\mathcal T_Q}\max_{(r,t)=1}|E(x,B;t,r)|
\ll \Psi(x,B)\frac{\varrho(Q/Y,B)}{\log Y}\,\varepsilon_N.
\tag{11}
\]

The uniformity checks are:

1. From (1), all smoothness densities used in this proof are \(B^{-O(1)}\), with uniform constants.
2. Therefore the large-conductor cutoff \(R_1=B^K\) has **fixed** \(K\).
3. The small-conductor transfer costs
   \[
   R_1^{1-\beta(Q/Y,B)}
   =\exp(O(K\log u))=(\log N)^{O(K)}.
   \]
4. The saving \(\exp(-cu/\log^2u)\) absorbs that factor, the FM grid, and any fixed power of \(d\).
5. The large-conductor terms
   \[
   B^{O(1)}\left(\frac{x}{Y}+x^{1/2}Q\right)
   \]
   are negligible relative to the right side’s main scale, by (9).

The external small-conductor input is exactly Drappeau, *Théorèmes de type Fouvry–Iwaniec pour les entiers friables*, **Compositio Math. 151 (2015), 828–862, Lemme 5**. Its constants and conductor range are absolute; taking \(A<1\) gives the stated effective implied constant. [Drappeau, Lemme 5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1307.7554)

At \(\alpha=2/5\),
\[
\log x\asymp D^{4/5}\lambda,\qquad
\log Y\asymp D^{3/5}\lambda,\qquad
\log B\asymp D^{2/5}\lambda.
\]
Thus \(Y\gg B^K\), while \(Y=x^{o(1)}\). There is no conflict with (9).

For a special-\(q\) interval of relative width \(1/d\), apply (11) to its containing dyadic interval and use positivity. This loses at most \(O(d)\) in the relative error, still absorbed by (10).

**Important qualification:** the three scales \(x,B,Y\) alone do not imply the lemma. A fixed relative level margin such as (9) is needed. It is feasible in this framework.

**1(c). Kern: an exponential constant is intrinsic at bare \(H=V^2\), but disappears with \(H\gg dV^2\).**

There is a concrete counterexample to a polynomial \(C_d\) under the literal hypothesis \(H\ge V^2\).

Take primes \(b=V\), \(b'<b-1\), with \(b'/b\to1\), and
\[
P=(1,b),\qquad P'=(1,b'),\qquad H=b^2.
\]
Put
\[
Q=(bx-y)(b'x-y).
\]
Every degree-\((d-3)\) form with coefficients in \(\{-1,0,1\}\), multiplied by \(Q\), lies in \(\ker\Phi\cap C_H\), because
\[
\|Q\|_1=bb'+b+b'+1=(b+1)(b'+1)<b^2.
\]
There are \(3^{d-2}\) distinct such vectors. Meanwhile
\[
\frac{\|v\wedge v'\|}{|\Delta|}
\ge (bb')^{d-2}.
\]
The proposed inequality therefore forces
\[
C_d\ge
\frac{3^{d-2}b^4(bb')^{d-2}}{(2b^2+1)^d}.
\]
Letting \(b\to\infty\) for each \(d\),
\[
C_d\ge \frac19(3/2)^d.
\tag{12}
\]
This concerns the uniform kernel lemma as stated; it does not assert that this example satisfies the special-\(q\) determinant condition.

There is nevertheless a simple polynomial-constant replacement.

The monomial basis of
\[
K=Q\,\mathbb Z[x,y]_{d-3}
\]
is banded: each coordinate receives contributions from at most three basis vectors. Its fundamental parallelepiped therefore has \(\ell^\infty\)-radius at most \(4V^2\), **independent of \(d\)**.

Let \(D_{\max}\) be the largest absolute \(2\times2\) minor of \([v;v']\). Projection onto the other \(d-2\) coordinates bounds every section of a cube of radius \(R\) by
\[
(2R)^{d-2}\frac{\|v\wedge v'\|}{D_{\max}}
\le d(2R)^{d-2}.
\]
Using \(\det K=\|v\wedge v'\|/|\Delta|\), disjoint fundamental cells give
\[
\Pr[\Phi(c)=z]
\ll
d\left(1+\frac{4V^2}{H}\right)^{d-2}
\frac{|\Delta|}{H^2\|v\wedge v'\|}.
\tag{13}
\]
Hence, for \(H\ge dV^2\),
\[
\Pr[\Phi(c)=z]\ll
d\,\frac{|\Delta|}{H^2\|v\wedge v'\|}.
\tag{14}
\]

The growing-degree parameters below already satisfy \(H/(dV^2)\to\infty\) rapidly.

**Verdict:** bare-height Kern has an intrinsic exponential loss; its \(\tau=1\) contribution is removable by a polynomial height increase, or simply by using the available height margin. The \(d^{O(d)}\) loss from the original cube enlargement is unnecessary.

**1(d). \(L2_{\rm fam}\): \(C_0\) can remain fixed, giving \(\tau=0\).**

Use the actual densities from (2), and replace the discretionary old slack \(L_N[1/2,1]\) in the cutoff definition by \(B\):
\[
R_0=B^{C_0}\ge
\frac{64S^2B}{\rho_r\rho_F}.
\tag{15}
\]
Because \(S^2=B^{O(1)}\) and \((\rho_r\rho_F)^{-1}=B^{O(1)}\), a fixed \(C_0\) suffices.

The dimension enters only through the sizes \(X_F,X_r\) and the comparisons ensuring that the large-sieve remainder is small. The DS losses are shifts by \(B^{O(C_0)}\), hence \((\log N)^{O(C_0)}\), with fixed exponent.

The four remainder ratios in the note become, up to fixed \(B\)-powers and polynomial factors,
\[
\frac{q}{R_0\rho_r\rho_F},\quad
N^{-h-(d+1)\nu}B^{O(1)},\quad
Y^{-1}B^{O(1)},\quad
N^{(5-d)\nu-h}B^{O(1)}.
\tag{16}
\]
They tend to zero for the explicit parameters below. Narrowing \(J\) multiplies the conductor-\(\ell\) terms by \(O(d)\), which changes nothing.

Keeping the obsolete \(L_N[1/2,1]\) cushion would unnecessarily force
\[
C_0\gtrsim D^{1/2-\alpha}\asymp d^{1/2}.
\]
That can manufacture a spurious \(\tau=1/2\) loss. The proof does not require this cushion.

**1(e). LD*: the “12 partners” is harmless; the wide geometric envelopes are not.**

The individual steps have the following dependence:

- **Kernel:** handled by (14).
- **AM–GM:** absolute factor only.
- **Prime multiplier:** Lemma W, discussed below.
- **Rational fibre DS:** fixed-\(C_0\) logarithmic loss.
- **Partners:** the bound \(12\) is absolute. It has no hidden \(d\) or \(k\).
- **Harmonic summation:** \(\sum_{q\le2S^2}\tau(q)/q\ll(1+\log S)^2\).
- **Geometric assembly:** this is where exponential losses occur.

With the original windows, two factors are genuinely present in the displayed upper bound:
\[
b^{-(d-2)}b'^{-(d-2)}
\le 2^{2d-4}V^{-2d+4},
\tag{17}
\]
and
\[
(V_{\max}/V_-)^{2d+O(1)}=\exp(O(d)).
\tag{18}
\]
They are multiplicative moment discrepancies. The smoothness-density adjustment for the corresponding \(2^{O(d)}\) argument shift is only \(1+o(1)\), so it cannot cancel them.

A sufficient design repair is
\[
b\in((1-1/d)V,V],\qquad
\ell\in(Y,(1+1/d)Y].
\tag{19}
\]
Then
\[
(bb')^{d-2}\gg V^{2d-4},\qquad
(V_{\max}/V_-)^{2d+O(1)}=O(1).
\]
The first moment loses only polynomial factors in \(d\). Ordinary effective PNT suffices to count primes in these intervals: their relative widths are inverse powers of \(\log N\), much larger than the PNT relative error at these sizes.

One further cleanup matters. Although the base expansion has an \(\exp(O(d))\) coefficient bound, its contribution is suppressed by \(N^{-\eta_0/2}\). Under (5), take
\[
X_F=C\,dMH V_{\max}^d
\tag{20}
\]
with absolute \(C\), rather than retaining an exponential \(C'_d\) in the final envelope.

After these changes,
\[
\mathbb E[N(N-1)]
\le d^{O(1)}(\log N)^C S^4\rho_r^2\rho_F^2.
\tag{21}
\]
Together with (7) and \(m\ge1\), this gives \(\gamma\ge(\log N)^{-C'}\), with absolute exponents after fixing the scaled parameters.

**Verdict:** literal wide-window assembly has \(\tau=1\); the repaired assembly has \(\tau=0\).

**1(f). Lemma W: \(\eta_0\to0\) by itself is insufficient.**

The actual Brun–Titchmarsh factor is
\[
C_W\le \frac{4\log W}{\log(W/t)}.
\tag{22}
\]
Thus one needs
\[
\log(W/t)>0,
\]
and, for the note’s convenient bound, \(\eta_0T\gg\log S\).

There is a precise failure if the slack shrinks too fast. With fixed \(\mu,\nu\), take \(\eta_0=T^{-2}\). Then
\[
W\asymp N^{\mu+\nu},\qquad t_{\max}\asymp N^{\mu+\nu}S.
\]
Choose a prime \(t\) near \(t_{\max}\). For any prime \(a\in(W,2W]\), the residue \(a\bmod t\) has probability about \(\log W/W\), whereas
\[
\frac{24/\eta_0}{\phi(t)}\asymp \frac{T^2}{t}.
\]
Their ratio tends to infinity like \(S\) divided by a polynomial. The claimed uniform Lemma W fails.

Conversely,
\[
\eta_0\asymp1/d,\qquad \eta_0T\gg\Lambda_\alpha+d\log d
\tag{23}
\]
works. Then \(24/\eta_0=O(d)\), so \(\tau=0\). With comparable \(\mu,\eta_0\), (22) is even bounded absolutely.

**1(g). Growing-\(k\) split counting: no additional precision exponent.**

The old
\[
c_k=8k^2\binom{2k}{k}
\]
is removed by S″. Its \(2^\omega\) loss is also removed, under the stated size condition.

To make the narrow special-\(q\) window compatible with products, choose
\[
Z=(\sqrt{N/8})^{1/k},\qquad
J=(Z/(1+1/d),Z]\cap\mathbb P.
\]
For a good set of relative density \(g\),
\[
n:=|G|\gg \frac{gZ}{d\log Z}.
\]
The \(k\)-products occupy at most \(2k\) dyadic product windows. Therefore some window contains
\[
|S|\ge \frac1{2k}\binom nk.
\]
S″ gives
\[
p_{\rm split}\ge
\frac{\binom nk^2}{8k^2\phi(N)},
\]
and hence
\[
\log\frac1{p_{\rm split}}
\le
2k\bigl(\log(1/g)+\log d+\log k+\log\log Z+O(1)\bigr)
+O(\log k).
\tag{24}
\]

The \(k!\) in \(\binom nk\) produces the usual \(O(k\log k)\) cost. It is an explicit split cost, not a loss subsequently raised to the power \(k\).

Also,
\[
\log |G|\asymp T/k,\qquad
\omega(N)\log2\le \frac{T\log2}{\log B}.
\]
At \(k\asymp D^{2/5}\), these differ by a factor \(\asymp\log\log N\), so \(|G|\ge4k^3 2^\omega\) holds for good fields.

Charge \(2kT_{\rm SQ}\) to every attempt. Then K(iii)’s waste ratio is unnecessary.

**2. Shared rational side**

**Conditional independence is exact, but insufficient on its own.** Given \(g\), the field vectors generated by independent \((w_j,c_j)\) are independent. Different targets \(\ell\) within one field need not be independent.

A counterexample to independence-only amplification is immediate: let every field succeed exactly on the same deterministic target subset of density \(\varepsilon\). The field vectors are independent—even deterministic—but any number of fields still covers density only \(\varepsilon\).

The following mixed-moment lemma supplies what is missing.

Let \(R(g,\ell)\) count rational-smooth candidates whose rational moduli are good for FM. Put
\[
r=S^2\rho_r,\qquad m=r\rho_F.
\]
Suppose, with \(P=(\log N)^C\),
\[
\mathbb ER\ge r/P,\qquad
\mathbb ER^2\le Pr^2,
\tag{25}
\]
and
\[
\mathbb E_{w,c}[N\mid g,\ell]\ge \rho_F R(g,\ell)/P.
\tag{26}
\]
Suppose further that
\[
\mathbb E_{g,\ell}\mathbb E_{w,c}[N^2\mid g,\ell]\le Km^2,
\qquad \log K=o(\Lambda_\alpha).
\tag{27}
\]

Then \(t=\exp(o(\Lambda_\alpha))\) fields sharing \(g\) give descendability density at least an inverse polynomial in \(\log N\).

*Proof.* From (25), a set of \((g,\ell)\)-cells of measure at least \(1/(4P^3)\) satisfies
\[
R\ge r/(2P).
\]
By Markov and (27), a subset of measure at least \(1/(8P^3)\) also satisfies
\[
\mathbb E[N^2\mid g,\ell]\le 24KP^3m^2.
\]
On this subset, (26) and conditional PZ give
\[
p(g,\ell):=\Pr_{w,c}[N>0\mid g,\ell]
\ge \frac1{96KP^7}.
\]
Taking \(t=\lceil96KP^7\rceil\), conditional independence gives coverage probability at least \(1-e^{-1}\) on this subset. Thus the averaged covered density is \(\gg P^{-3}\). ∎

These premises are available from the repaired arguments:

- (25): rational FM and Theorem R; removing bad moduli changes the first moment negligibly.
- (26): the pointwise good-modulus coefficient argument.
- (27): even the **coarse** LD* bound suffices when its loss is \(\exp(O(d\log d))=\exp(o(\Lambda_\alpha))\).

Thus no uniform-in-\(g\) second-moment theorem is required. A conditional mean plus an averaged conditional second moment is enough.

Here “\(L^{-o(1)}\)” must mean relative to **\(L_N[\alpha,1]\)**. An unspecified \(L_N[1/2,1]^{-o(1)}\) loss is insufficient.

There is a post-processing repair too. With \(t\) fields, there can be \(t\) residual exceptional bits, so three dependency tests no longer suffice. Choose \(t+1\) independent kernel vectors. Exact square tests in each field label these vectors by \(t\) binary residual coordinates; solve the resulting binary system and obtain a nonzero combination square in every field. Verification remains exact. All additional work and columns contribute \(\exp(o(\Lambda_\alpha))\).

**Conclusion:** the shared-side trick can remove algebraic precision losses. It does not change the one-level norm geometry, and therefore does not get below \(2/5\).

**3. LV6 at growing degree**

The factorial issue in Lemma X is real. Stark’s relevant result is **Lemma 8**, *Some effective cases of the Brauer–Siegel theorem*, **Invent. Math. 23 (1974), 135–152**, with \(c_1(n)=4n!\) in the non-normal case. [Stark, Lemma 8](https://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/download/pdf/PPN356556735_0023/PPN356556735_0023.pdf)

Put
\[
\nu_1\asymp\frac1{(2d)!\log\bar\Delta},\qquad s=\log x.
\]
The original argument requires
\[
\nu_1s\gg\log s
\tag{28}
\]
to suppress the exceptional term relative to \(x/\log x\).

**Factorial-bit repair: valid.** For example,
\[
s=(2d)!\,(T+\log\bar\Delta+d)^4
\tag{29}
\]
satisfies (28) and the ordinary Chebotarev error requirements. It also repairs the first-degree-prime sampler for the odd-degree base field.

Using algorithms polynomial in \(d,s\) with absolute exponents, the resulting overhead is
\[
\exp(O(d\log d))\,T^{O(1)}.
\tag{30}
\]
Since \(d\asymp D^{1-2\alpha}\), this is \(L_N[\alpha]^{o(1)}\) whenever \(\alpha>1/3\), including \(2/5\).

The other growing-degree costs are similarly harmless:

- \(\log|H|\) is polynomial in \(d,T\); retaining the actual height bounds gives \(O(dT)\) in the balanced regime.
- The \(2^{d-1}\) square-root sign enumeration costs \(\exp(O(d))\).
- Coefficient bounds in SQ affect bit lengths polynomially.
- IR can be uniform: its finite-field counting argument works with an absolute lower prime threshold. Its rejection bound is
  \[
  \exp\!\left(-\Omega\!\left(\frac{\log H}{d\log\log H}\right)\right),
  \]
  negligible here.

**Polynomial-bit alternative: also valid, with potentially factorially many tests.**

Use Thorner–Zaman, *A unified and improved Chebotarev density theorem*, **Algebra & Number Theory 13 (2019), 1039–1068, Theorem 1.1**—or its abelian refinement, Theorem 1.4. Its error is relative to
\[
\operatorname{Li}(x)-\theta_1\operatorname{Li}(x^{\beta_1}),
\]
including when that expression is small. [Thorner–Zaman 2019](https://msp.org/ant/2019/13-5/ant-v13-n5-p02-s.pdf)

Take
\[
s=(T+\log\bar\Delta+d)^4.
\]
For \(h\notin E\), every possible exceptional zero has \(1-\beta_1\ge\nu_1\). The theorem gives, on the dyadic interval,
\[
\#\{\text{inert degree-one primes}\}
\gg \min(1,\nu_1s)\frac{x}{\log x}.
\]
Higher-degree primes are negligible. Comparing with the elementary upper bound \(O(dx/\log x)\), the detection probability is
\[
\gg \frac{\min(1,\nu_1s)}d.
\tag{31}
\]
Thus polynomial-bit character primes suffice with
\[
\exp(O(d\log d))\,T^{O(1)}
\]
sampling and character overhead. The same reasoning repairs the base-field sampler.

This is enough for the factoring exponent. It is **not** a claim of polynomial total overhead.

For comparison, the earlier lower bound in Thorner–Zaman, **Algebra & Number Theory 11 (2017), 1135–1197, Theorem 3.1**, includes a factor \((D_KQn_K^{n_K})^{-5}\). Used directly, that density is far too small. A least-prime theorem alone likewise does not justify efficient random sampling. [Thorner–Zaman 2017, Theorem 3.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1604.01750)

**4. Literature**

I found no verified classical theorem giving \(L_N[\alpha,c]\), \(\alpha<1/2\), for factoring arbitrary \(N\), unconditionally or with hypotheses confined to primality testing.

The established general bound remains:

H. W. Lenstra Jr. and C. Pomerance, **“A rigorous time bound for factoring integers,” J. Amer. Math. Soc. 5 (1992), 483–516**, expected \(L_N[1/2,1+o(1)]\). [Original paper](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf)

Harvey’s **“An exponent one-fifth algorithm for deterministic integer factorisation,” Math. Comp. 90 (2021), 2937–2950**, explicitly identifies this as the best rigorous probabilistic bound. [Harvey](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.05450)

LV does not supply a counterexample: its unconditional conclusions concern congruences of squares. Its factoring result, Theorem 2.3, assumes **Conjecture 7.1** and concerns a restricted semiprime class. That is an additional number-theoretic hypothesis, unrelated to primality testing. [LV, Theorem 2.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

